{"text": "The Lancet : Saturday 28 January 1989\n\nPLACEBO-CONTROLLED, RANDOMISED\nTRIAL OF WARFARIN AND ASPIRIN FOR\nPREVENTION OF THROMBOEMBOLIC\nCOMPLICATIONS IN CHRONIC ATRIAL\nFIBRILLATION\n\n\u2018The Copenhagen AFASAK Study\n\nPALLE PETERSEN GUDRUN Boysen\nJouN GoDTFREDSEN ELLEN D. ANDERSEN\nBJORN ANDERSEN\n\nDiperonen of Nawab, Unicrsty Hospital, Rgshopitalet,\n9 Blane, DK-2100, Copenhagen, Denmark\nSummary FromNovember, 1985,t0 June, 1988, 1007\n\u2018outpatients with\u2019 chronic non-rheumatic\n\u2018ural fibrilition (AF) entered a randomised trial; 335\nrecived anticoagulation with warfarin openly, and in a\n<buble-blind study 336 received aspirin 75 mg once daily\n\u2018211386 placebo, Each patient was followed up for 2 years oF\n\u2018ntl termination ofthe trial. The primary endpoint was a\nthromboembolic complication (stroke, transient cerebral\nichuemicattack, or embolic complications tothe visceraand\n\u2018xemties). The secondary endpoint was death. The\n\u2018incidence of thromboembolic complications and vascular\n\u2018morality were significantly lower inthe warfarin group than\nin the aspirin and placebo groups, which did not differ\nsigncanly. 5 patents on warfarin had thromboembolic\ncomplications compared with 20 patients on aspirin and 21\n\u2018placebo? patients on warfarin were withdrawn because\nof nonfatal bleeding complications compared with 2 on\naspirin and none on placebo, \u2018Thus, anticoagulation therapy\n\u2018wth warfrin can be recommended to prevent\nthemombiscompiatons in patents with chron\non-theumatic A\n\nIntroduction\n\nARAL fibrillation (AF) is complicated by a high risk of\n\u2018thromboembolic complications. Chronic AF also implies\nhigh risk of \u2018clinically silent cerebral infarction.*\nParoxysmal AF, however is associated with a lower risk of\nstroke.* To our knowledge, no randomised study of\n\u2018prophylaxis with anticoagulants or aspirin has been done in\npatients with chronic non-theumatic AF, although the\n\u2018question of whether to use stich treatment has been debated\nfor decades\"\n\u201cThe aim of this randomised trial was to compare the\neffects of warfarin anticoagulation, low-dose aspirin\ntherapy, and placebo on the incidence of thromboembolic\n\u2018complications in patients with chronic non-rheumatic AF.\n\nPatients and Methods\n\n\u201cThe stody was carried out between Nov 1, 1985,and June, 1988\n(dhe Copenhagen AFASAK study). The patients were recruited\nfrom to outpatient elecrocardiography (ECG) laboratories to\nwhich they hod been refered by their general pratconers.\n\u2018Whenever AF was diagnosed, we informed te general practioner\nabout the study IPhe or she agreed, the patent was invited take\nprin the study. After giving informed consent, the patent was\n\u2018amined by one of us (PP), who measured blood pressure and\nheart ate and carried out echocardiography to determine eft atrial\nsae: The history taken covered previeus sheumatc fever, chest,\npia, symptoms of heat failure, myocirdial infarction, and\n\u2018crebrotascular episodes. Laboratory investigations included a\n1Bead ECG, chest X-ray with determination of relative heart\n\u2018volume, and haemoglobin, serum concentrations of sodium and\npotassium, blond glucose, platelet count, caaglation sas, and\ntests of hepatic renal, and thyroid function. Diagnostic extra on\nthe underving aetiology of AF have been described previously? To\nbe included inthe study\u2019 patients had wo be 18 years of age oF over\nsand tohave ECG- verified chronic AF, The exchson criteria were!\nprevious anticoagulation therapy for more than 6. months;\nerebrovascular event within the past month; contraindication or",
